---
// src/components/AchievementIslandSection.astro
//
// Same family as ComputerIslandSection.astro: two flanking
// illustrations sitting behind centered-ish text, one JS-driven
// mouse-repel script, one CSS bob loop per island. Neither island
// bleeds off the section's edges here either, so positioning is plain
// vw/vh (same reasoning as ComputerIslandSection.astro — a vw/vh
// offset already scales continuously with the viewport, so there's no
// need for the clamp(mobileValue, calc(a + b*vw), desktopValue) trick
// ArtIslandSection.astro needs for its bleeding corners).
//
// Per your reference screenshot, the arrangement is close to
// ComputerIslandSection's but tuned differently:
//
//   - `leftImage`  (statue island, svg) -> mid-left, narrower and
//     taller than the telephone-pole island was, sitting a little
//     further in from the edge.
//   - `rightImage` (temple island, svg) -> right side, large, sitting
//     lower and tucked in much closer to the right edge than the
//     computer island was.
//
// (The mountain shape bottom-left in your screenshot is the
// persistent CornerIsland.astro front island that's on every section
// via Layout.astro — not one of this component's two images.)
//
// Included a mobile media query mirroring ComputerIslandSection's:
// left island drops into normal flow below the text (via `order`)
// instead of overlaying it, right island gets a tighter `right` so it
// doesn't creep under the now full-width text. Same as the other
// sections in this family, treat these as starting values to nudge
// once you see it live.
//
// Background: was a real image (`backgroundImage`) via `background-
// image` + `background-size: cover`, same technique every section in
// this family used. New: swapped for a flat `background-color:
// #2ddcfc;` on `.achievement-section` per your request — no image to
// crop or position any more, so `background-size`/`background-
// position`/`background-repeat` and the `backgroundImage` prop are
// gone entirely (same kind of swap ComputerIslandSection.astro's
// gradient background went through earlier). `min-height`/`aspect-
// ratio` below are UNCHANGED by this — they were always about this
// section's own SHAPE (how tall it grows on unusual viewports), a
// separate concern from what paints inside it, so a flat color still
// benefits from the section never getting squashed shorter than
// 16:9 on a short/wide viewport, exactly as the image did.
//
// No seam-fade classes any more — the shared fade-band system that
// used to blend section edges together was removed per your request,
// so this section now just butts directly against ComputerIslandSection
// above it and BonusIslandSection below it.
//
// Still carries `.sky-chain`, so the gap-collapsing on both sides
// keeps working regardless of what backs it.
//
// Carries `.halftone-hover`, joining the single, page-wide cursor-
// spotlight element in Layout.astro (`.halftone-spotlight`, see the
// comment there) purely by having this class — no markup or script
// needed here for it, since `body:has(.halftone-hover:hover)
// .halftone-spotlight` is what actually turns the spotlight on.
//
// Two optional "front chunk" images — `rightChunk1Image` /
// `rightChunk2Image` (your right_achievement_island_first_front_
// chunk.webp / right_achievement_island_second_front_chunk.webp) —
// smaller rock fragments that sit in front of and below the main
// temple island, per your reference screenshot: chunk 1 (the bigger
// one) tucked just under-left of the temple's base, chunk 2 (smaller)
// further down-left, partly overlapping chunk 1. Positioned with the
// same plain vw/vh approach as the two main islands, as starting
// values eyeballed off your screenshot — nudge once it's live, same
// as everything else in this family.
//
// Both chunks get the SAME bob-loop + mouse-repel treatment as
// `leftImage`/`rightImage` (own `.achievement-glitch` outer wrapper
// for the CSS bob animation, `.achievement-glitch-inner.parallax-
// inner` child for the JS drift — the existing script below already
// picks these up for free, since it queries `.parallax-inner`
// globally like every other section in this family). What's new is
// that they're NOT always visible: per your reference (the small
// symbols/pictures that flicker in and out as you move the cursor on
// rossdraws.com), each chunk is normally invisible and only glitches
// into view when the cursor is close to where it sits — moving away
// lets it linger for a couple seconds before it hides again, rather
// than vanishing the instant the cursor leaves, and it keeps
// re-glitching at random intervals for as long as it lingers instead
// of flashing once and then sitting there static. Each glitch plays a
// quick "digital tear" flourish (a banded `clip-path` jump-cut plus a
// brief hue-rotate flash, both using `steps()` easing so the
// transition SNAPS between states instead of smoothly tweening) and a
// scanline texture overlay that's only present mid-flash.
//
// Two optional `*Glitched` images — `leftImageGlitched` (your
// left_achievement_island_glitched.webp) for the statue, and
// `rightImageGlitched` (your right_achievements_island_glitched.webp)
// for the temple. Same cursor-proximity trigger and same glitch
// flourish as the two chunks, but a different effect — instead of an
// element popping in/out of existence, this CROSSFADES an island from
// its normal image to its glitched variant and back, i.e. the island
// is always there, only its texture glitches. Implemented as two
// stacked images inside a new `.achievement-glitch-swap` wrapper
// (same nested-stack technique as `.intro-cloud-inner`/`.cloud-smile`
// in IntroIslandSection.astro), used identically for both islands:
// the normal image sits in normal flow and is always visible; the
// glitched image is absolutely positioned on top of it at
// `opacity: 0`, and only shows (with the same glitch-pop/glitch-scan
// animations the chunks use) while `.is-glitched` is applied. Either
// prop can be passed on its own — an island without its `*Glitched`
// prop just renders its normal image with no swap behavior, same as
// before this existed.
//
// The proximity check that drives all of this (both chunks + both
// island swaps) is one shared `watchGlitchReveal()` helper in the
// script below, rather than four copies of near-identical logic —
// they're the same shape (watch a distance, toggle a class, re-glitch
// on an interval while in range, linger after leaving) and only
// differ in which element the class goes on and how the reveal radius
// is computed. The two island swaps specifically are discovered
// generically (every `.achievement-glitch-swap` on the page that
// actually has a `.achievement-glitch-swap-alt` inside it), rather
// than being wired up by hand per island — distance for each is
// measured from its own closest `.achievement-island` ancestor (the
// whole island's footprint, not just the swap wrapper), so hovering
// anywhere near an island triggers it, and the radius scales with
// THAT island's own rendered size rather than a shared fixed value,
// since the statue (`clamp(130px, 12vw, 200px)` wide) and the temple
// (`clamp(320px, 40vw, 560px)` wide) are very different sizes.
//
// `pointer-events: none` stays on `.achievement-glitch`/`.achievement-
// island` (same as every other island) since these are purely
// decorative and `aria-hidden`, so none of this can use native
// `:hover`/`mouseenter` — those require the element to actually
// receive pointer events. The distance-based JS check works around
// that without needing anything here to be interactive.
//
// Both chunks and the temple swap default to `z-index: 1` (or inherit
// the temple island's own z-index 1, in the swap's case), same tier
// as `.achievement-island` — this matters for the halftone spotlight
// in Layout.astro: that element sits at `z-index: 0` specifically so
// everything at `z-index: 1`+ paints over it, untouched by its
// dot-pattern blend. Keeping everything here at that same tier means
// it's excluded from the halftone tint exactly like the other islands
// are, rather than (accidentally) being treated as part of the
// background.
//
// Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`: both chunks are shown
// statically (no glitch animation, no scanline texture, no
// proximity-gating) — popping in and out as the cursor moves is
// exactly the kind of motion that setting is meant to suppress, so
// this shows them plainly instead of just hiding them outright. The
// temple swap needs no special handling for this — its default state
// (no `.is-glitched`) already shows the normal `rightImage`, so
// simply never attaching its proximity watcher under reduced motion
// leaves it showing the normal image permanently, which is exactly
// what's wanted.
//
// On narrow screens (`max-width: 640px`) both chunks are hidden
// entirely rather than repositioned — they're purely decorative
// flourish, not core content, and there isn't a clean spot for them
// once the layout collapses to the mobile single-column treatment.
// The temple swap stays active on mobile (it doesn't add any new
// layout footprint, just crossfades in place) — though in practice it
// won't trigger there anyway, since touch devices have no persistent
// cursor to be "near" the island in the first place.
// Ask if you'd rather the chunks show up (smaller, repositioned) on
// mobile too.
//
// NOTE: I still don't have the actual chunk/glitch image files for
// this section — this is reasoned from first principles, not
// measured against the real files. (The background itself is no
// longer an image, see above.)
//
// Usage:
//   <AchievementIslandSection
//     id="section-achievements"
//     eyebrow="Island 04"
//     title="Do Eiusmod Tempor"
//     text="Nemo enim ipsam voluptatem quia voluptas sit aspernatur..."
//     leftImage="/images/left_achievement_island.svg"
//     leftImageGlitched="/images/left_achievement_island_glitched.webp"
//     rightImage="/images/right_achievement_island.svg"
//     rightImageGlitched="/images/right_achievements_island_glitched.webp"
//     rightChunk1Image="/images/right_achievement_island_first_front_chunk.webp"
//     rightChunk2Image="/images/right_achievement_island_second_front_chunk.webp"
//   />

interface Props {
  id: string;
  eyebrow?: string;
  title: string;
  text: string;
  leftImage: string;
  leftImageGlitched?: string;
  rightImage: string;
  rightImageGlitched?: string;
  rightChunk1Image?: string;
  rightChunk2Image?: string;
}

const {
  id,
  eyebrow,
  title,
  text,
  leftImage,
  leftImageGlitched,
  rightImage,
  rightImageGlitched,
  rightChunk1Image,
  rightChunk2Image,
} = Astro.props;
---

<section
  class="achievement-section sky-chain halftone-hover"
  id={id}
>
  <div class="achievement-island achievement-island-left" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="achievement-island-inner parallax-inner">
      <div class="achievement-glitch-swap">
        <img class="achievement-glitch-swap-base" src={leftImage} alt="" />
        {leftImageGlitched && (
          <div class="achievement-glitch-swap-alt">
            <img src={leftImageGlitched} alt="" />
          </div>
        )}
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="content">
    {eyebrow && <p class="eyebrow">{eyebrow}</p>}
    <h2>{title}</h2>
    <p class="body-text">{text}</p>
  </div>

  <div class="achievement-island achievement-island-right" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="achievement-island-inner parallax-inner">
      <div class="achievement-glitch-swap">
        <img class="achievement-glitch-swap-base" src={rightImage} alt="" />
        {rightImageGlitched && (
          <div class="achievement-glitch-swap-alt">
            <img src={rightImageGlitched} alt="" />
          </div>
        )}
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  {rightChunk1Image && (
    <div class="achievement-glitch achievement-glitch-1" aria-hidden="true">
      <div class="achievement-glitch-inner parallax-inner">
        <img src={rightChunk1Image} alt="" />
      </div>
    </div>
  )}

  {rightChunk2Image && (
    <div class="achievement-glitch achievement-glitch-2" aria-hidden="true">
      <div class="achievement-glitch-inner parallax-inner">
        <img src={rightChunk2Image} alt="" />
      </div>
    </div>
  )}
</section>

<script>
  // Same proximity-repel drift as the rest of this family, driving
  // every `.parallax-inner` element on the page. If more than one of
  // these sections lands on the same page, each ships its own copy of
  // this script and each queries ALL `.parallax-inner` elements
  // globally (not just its own) — intentional/harmless (they all
  // compute the same easing toward the same targets), just a little
  // redundant. Worth hoisting into one shared script if this family
  // keeps growing.
  const islands = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(".parallax-inner");
  const prefersReducedMotion = window.matchMedia(
    "(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)"
  ).matches;

  if (islands.length && !prefersReducedMotion) {
    const maxOffset = 36; // px, strongest push when cursor is right on top of the island
    const influenceRadius = 500; // px, push fades to 0 beyond this distance
    const ease = 0.08;

    let mouseX = 0;
    let mouseY = 0;
    let hasMouse = false;

    const state = Array.from(islands).map((el) => ({
      el,
      currentX: 0,
      currentY: 0,
    }));

    window.addEventListener("mousemove", (e) => {
      mouseX = e.clientX;
      mouseY = e.clientY;
      hasMouse = true;
    });

    function tick() {
      for (const s of state) {
        let targetX = 0;
        let targetY = 0;

        if (hasMouse) {
          const rect = s.el.getBoundingClientRect();
          const restX = rect.left + rect.width / 2 - s.currentX;
          const restY = rect.top + rect.height / 2 - s.currentY;

          const dx = restX - mouseX;
          const dy = restY - mouseY;
          const dist = Math.hypot(dx, dy) || 1;

          if (dist < influenceRadius) {
            const falloff = 1 - dist / influenceRadius;
            const nx = dx / dist;
            const ny = dy / dist;
            targetX = nx * maxOffset * falloff;
            targetY = ny * maxOffset * falloff;
          }
        }

        s.currentX += (targetX - s.currentX) * ease;
        s.currentY += (targetY - s.currentY) * ease;
        s.el.style.transform = `translate(${s.currentX.toFixed(2)}px, ${s.currentY.toFixed(2)}px)`;
      }
      requestAnimationFrame(tick);
    }

    requestAnimationFrame(tick);
  }

  // Shared cursor-proximity "glitch reveal" watcher — used below for
  // both the two front rock chunks (whole-element show/hide) and the
  // temple island's normal/glitched image crossfade. Both cases boil
  // down to the same shape: watch the distance from the cursor to
  // some element's static rest position, and toggle a class on some
  // (possibly different) element once that distance crosses a radius
  // — reveal immediately + keep re-glitching on a random interval
  // while in range, then linger for `hideDelay` after the cursor
  // leaves before reverting, cancelling that linger if the cursor
  // comes back in time. One shared function instead of duplicating
  // this per use, since the only real differences between the chunks
  // and the temple swap are WHICH class gets toggled, WHERE the
  // distance is measured from/to, and the reveal radius.
  //
  // `radius` can be a plain number (the two chunks: small, roughly
  // fixed-size images) or a function of the watched element's own
  // `DOMRect` (the temple island: its rendered size ranges 320-560px
  // wide across viewports via its own `clamp()`, so a single fixed px
  // radius would feel too tight at the large end or too generous at
  // the small end).
  function watchGlitchReveal(
    targets: {
      watch: HTMLElement; // element whose rest position distance is measured from
      toggle: HTMLElement; // element the class is actually added/removed on
      radius: number | ((rect: DOMRect) => number);
    }[],
    className: string,
    options: { hideDelay: number; glitchIntervalMin: number; glitchIntervalMax: number }
  ) {
    const state = targets.map((t) => ({
      ...t,
      visible: false,
      hideTimeout: null as ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null,
      glitchTimeout: null as ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null,
    }));

    // Force a reflow before re-adding the class so the one-shot
    // `glitch-pop`/`glitch-scan` CSS animations restart from frame 0
    // every time, rather than silently not restarting because the
    // class never really left the "applied" state from the browser's
    // point of view. Used both for the initial reveal and for every
    // re-glitch while something lingers on screen below.
    function playGlitch(el: HTMLElement) {
      el.classList.remove(className);
      void el.offsetWidth;
      el.classList.add(className);
    }

    // While something is visible, keep re-triggering the glitch flash
    // at random intervals — the initial reveal already plays one,
    // this just keeps it looking "unstable" for as long as it
    // lingers, instead of the animation playing once and then sitting
    // there static for the rest of `hideDelay`. Stops rescheduling
    // itself the moment it's hidden.
    function scheduleGlitch(s: (typeof state)[number]) {
      const delay =
        options.glitchIntervalMin +
        Math.random() * (options.glitchIntervalMax - options.glitchIntervalMin);
      s.glitchTimeout = setTimeout(() => {
        if (s.visible) {
          playGlitch(s.toggle);
          scheduleGlitch(s);
        }
      }, delay);
    }

    window.addEventListener("mousemove", (e) => {
      for (const s of state) {
        const rect = s.watch.getBoundingClientRect();
        const cx = rect.left + rect.width / 2;
        const cy = rect.top + rect.height / 2;
        const dist = Math.hypot(e.clientX - cx, e.clientY - cy);
        const radius = typeof s.radius === "function" ? s.radius(rect) : s.radius;
        const shouldShow = dist < radius;

        if (shouldShow) {
          // Cursor's back in range (or never left) — cancel any
          // pending hide so a brief dip out of range doesn't cut the
          // lingering period short.
          if (s.hideTimeout !== null) {
            clearTimeout(s.hideTimeout);
            s.hideTimeout = null;
          }

          if (!s.visible) {
            s.visible = true;
            playGlitch(s.toggle);
            scheduleGlitch(s);
          }
        } else if (s.visible && s.hideTimeout === null) {
          // Cursor just left range — don't hide immediately, let it
          // linger for `hideDelay` first. If the cursor comes back
          // before that fires, the branch above cancels it.
          s.hideTimeout = setTimeout(() => {
            s.visible = false;
            s.hideTimeout = null;
            if (s.glitchTimeout !== null) {
              clearTimeout(s.glitchTimeout);
              s.glitchTimeout = null;
            }
            s.toggle.classList.remove(className);
          }, options.hideDelay);
        }
      }
    });
  }

  const glitchTiming = { hideDelay: 2500, glitchIntervalMin: 500, glitchIntervalMax: 1400 };

  // Front rock chunks (see `.achievement-glitch` in the <style>
  // below): whole-element show/hide, `.is-visible` toggled on the
  // same element the distance is measured from.
  const glitchChunks = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(".achievement-glitch");

  if (glitchChunks.length) {
    if (prefersReducedMotion) {
      // Show plainly, no glitch/texture/proximity-gating — see the
      // component-level comment for why this shows the chunks instead
      // of just leaving them hidden.
      for (const chunk of glitchChunks) {
        chunk.classList.add("is-visible", "no-motion");
      }
    } else {
      watchGlitchReveal(
        Array.from(glitchChunks).map((el) => ({ watch: el, toggle: el, radius: 180 })),
        "is-visible",
        glitchTiming
      );
    }
  }

  // Island glitch swaps (see `.achievement-glitch-swap` in the
  // <style> below): crossfades an island's normal image to its
  // glitched variant and back, instead of showing/hiding a whole
  // element like the chunks do. Handles however many
  // `.achievement-glitch-swap` instances exist — currently the
  // statue (`leftImageGlitched`) and the temple
  // (`rightImageGlitched`) — generically rather than querying each
  // one by hand, so a third island picking this up later needs no
  // script changes, only the same markup pattern. Each swap's
  // distance is measured from its own closest `.achievement-island`
  // ancestor (the whole island's footprint, not just the swap
  // wrapper), so hovering anywhere near an island triggers it, and
  // only swaps that actually have a `.achievement-glitch-swap-alt`
  // (i.e. a glitched image was actually passed for that island) get a
  // watcher at all. Radius scales with each island's OWN rendered
  // size rather than a shared fixed px value, since island sizes vary
  // a lot in this family (the statue is
  // `clamp(130px, 12vw, 200px)` wide, the temple
  // `clamp(320px, 40vw, 560px)`). No `prefers-reduced-motion` branch
  // needed — every swap's default (no `.is-glitched`) already shows
  // its normal image, so simply not attaching a watcher leaves it
  // that way.
  if (!prefersReducedMotion) {
    const islandSwapTargets = Array.from(
      document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(".achievement-glitch-swap")
    )
      .filter((swap) => swap.querySelector(".achievement-glitch-swap-alt"))
      .map((swap) => ({
        watch: swap.closest<HTMLElement>(".achievement-island") ?? swap,
        toggle: swap,
        radius: (rect: DOMRect) => Math.max(rect.width, rect.height) / 2 + 40,
      }));

    if (islandSwapTargets.length) {
      watchGlitchReveal(islandSwapTargets, "is-glitched", glitchTiming);
    }
  }
</script>

<style>
  .achievement-section {
    position: relative;
    min-height: 100vh;
    /* `aspect-ratio: 16 / 9` matches the background image (measured at
       3840x2160) — combined with `width: 100%` and `min-height`, the
       browser uses whichever constraint needs MORE height: on a
       viewport wider-than-16:9 (mainly short/wide windows), the
       aspect-ratio wins and this section grows taller than one
       screen, showing the FULL image at its native ratio with no
       cropping — you scroll to see the rest, per your request,
       instead of `cover` shortening (cropping) the image's height to
       fit a too-short viewport. On a taller-than-16:9 viewport (e.g.
       mobile portrait), `min-height: 100vh` wins instead, so the
       section stays exactly one screen tall and `cover` crops the
       sides only — unchanged from before. `width: 100%` is required
       alongside `aspect-ratio` — without it, on the min-height-wins
       branch the browser would stretch this section's WIDTH to
       satisfy the ratio instead of its height, overflowing past the
       viewport edges. */
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    padding: clamp(3rem, 8vw, 7rem) clamp(1.5rem, 6vw, 6rem);
    /* No `overflow: hidden` here — see the comment on `overflow-x:
       hidden` on `html` in Layout.astro. That handles horizontal
       clipping page-wide, so an island that needs more vertical room
       than this section provides isn't hard-clipped. */
    /* New: flat color instead of an image (see the top-of-file
       comment) — nothing to crop or position, so `background-size`/
       `background-position`/`background-repeat` are gone; a plain
       `background-color` always paints at this section's own
       furthest-back layer regardless of any child's z-index, same as
       ComputerIslandSection's gradient. */
    background-color: #2ddcfc;
  }

  .content {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 2;

    width: min(32ch, 100%);
    margin: 0 auto;

    text-align: left;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #555;
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    color: #1a1a1a;
  }

  .body-text {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: #444;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .achievement-island {
    position: absolute;
    z-index: 1;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .achievement-island-inner img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  /* Statue island: narrower and taller than a typical flanking island,
     mid-left but a bit further in from the edge than
     ComputerIslandSection's telephone-pole island was. Doesn't bleed
     off any edge. */
  .achievement-island-left {
    top: 30vh;
    left: 22vw;
    width: clamp(130px, 12vw, 200px);
    animation: bob-left 6.5s ease-in-out infinite;
  }

  /* Temple island: large, sitting lower and tucked in much closer to
     the right edge than the computer island was. Also doesn't bleed
     off any edge. */
  .achievement-island-right {
    top: 16vh;
    right: 2vw;
    width: clamp(320px, 40vw, 560px);
    animation: bob-right 5.8s ease-in-out infinite;
  }

  @keyframes bob-left {
    0%, 100% { transform: translateY(0); }
    50% { transform: translateY(-10px); }
  }

  @keyframes bob-right {
    0%, 100% { transform: translateY(0); }
    50% { transform: translateY(-8px); }
  }

  /* Temple glitch swap: the normal and glitched temple images stacked
     on top of each other, same nested-stack technique as
     `.intro-cloud-inner`/`.cloud-smile` in IntroIslandSection.astro.
     `position: relative` so the absolutely positioned alt image lines
     up exactly over the base one. Sized entirely by the base image
     (normal document flow, `width: 100%; height: auto` via the
     existing `.achievement-island-inner img` rule below) — the alt
     image and its wrapper just stretch to match via `inset: 0`. */
  .achievement-glitch-swap {
    position: relative;
  }

  .achievement-glitch-swap-alt {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    opacity: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .achievement-glitch-swap.is-glitched .achievement-glitch-swap-alt {
    opacity: 1;
